package compoent

import "testing"

func Test_Thinking(t *testing.T) {
	c := NewThinkingMessage("123", "")
	for i := 0; i < len(content)-1; i = i + 10 {
		c.SetContent(content[:i])
		c.Render(80)
	}
}

var content = "Now I have a thorough understanding of the entire codebase. Here is my comprehensive bug analysis:\n\n---\n\n## 🐛 Comprehensive Bug Analysis for GoCode\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL (Will Crash)\n\n#### 1. Debug Panics Left in Production — `thinking.go:82-85`\n\n```go\nif util.HasTrimEmptyLine(newOut) {\n    panic(\"hahahaha\")\n}\nif util.HasTrimEmptyLine(newOut) {\n    panic(\"yayayayaya\")\n}\n```\n\nThese are debug/testing panics accidentally left in `ThinkingMessage.renderMarkdown()`. If the rendered markdown has a trailing empty line, **the program will crash**. Since markdown rendering can easily produce trailing empty lines, this is a **real and likely crash scenario**.\n\n**Fix:** Delete these two `if` blocks.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. `ReconstructHistory` Will Panic on Malformed History — `agent.go:259,269`\n\n```go\ncase string(MsgToolCall):\n    if pendingAssistant.Content == \"\" \u0026\u0026 pendingAssistant.ReasoningContent == \"\" {\n        panic(`pendingAssistant.Content == \"\" \u0026\u0026 ...`)\n    }\ncase string(MsgToolResult):\n    if len(pendingAssistant.ToolCalls) == 0 {\n        panic(`pendingAssistant.ToolCalls == 0`)\n    }\n```\n\nIf the stored session file has a `tool_call` or `tool_result` message without a preceding `assistant` or `thinking` message (e.g., due to file corruption, manual editing, or a subtle ordering bug), the program panics instead of handling it gracefully.\n\n**Fix:** Return an error or skip the orphaned message instead of panicking.\n\n---\n\n### 🟠 HIGH (Data Loss / Incorrect Behavior)\n\n#### 3. Non-Stable Sort Breaks Message Order on Load — `store.go:108-110`\n\n```go\nsort.Slice(s.messages[sessionID], func(i, j int) bool {\n    return s.messages[sessionID][i].CreatedAt \u003c s.messages[sessionID][j].CreatedAt\n})\n```\n\n`sort.Slice` is **not stable**. If two messages have the same `CreatedAt` timestamp (which happens when `MsgToolCall` and `MsgToolResult` are persisted rapidly, or when `MsgAssistant` and `MsgThinking` final callbacks fire simultaneously), their relative order may be swapped after sorting. A `MsgToolResult` appearing before its corresponding `MsgToolCall` would cause `ReconstructHistory` to either panic (see bug #2) or silently produce corrupted context.\n\n**Fix:** Use `sort.SliceStable` instead.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. `PersistMessage` Saves Both ToolCall AND ToolResult with Same MsgID — `agent.go` in TUI\n\nThe `persistMessage` function saves `MsgToolCall` and `MsgToolResult` as separate messages with the **same** `MsgID`. On reload, `LoadSession` processes them sequentially (tool call creates the component, tool result updates it). But since they are separate stored messages, `ReconstructHistory` processes them independently. If only one of the pair is present (e.g., crash mid-write), the reconstruction panics.\n\nAdditionally, the duplicate `MsgID` means `AppendMessage` stores both messages. On reload, two history entries with the same ID exist. This works because the switch handles them sequentially, but it's fragile.\n\n**Fix:** Consider merging ToolCall+ToolResult into a single persisted message, or ensuring the order is always preserved without relying on sort stability.\n\n---\n\n### 🟡 MEDIUM (Incorrect / Unexpected Behavior)\n\n#### 5. `runShellCommand` Always Returns `nil` Error — `tools.go:236-252`\n\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    return fmt.Sprintf(\"exit %v\\n%s\", err, result), nil  // ← always nil\n}\n// ...\nreturn fmt.Sprintf(\"(timed out after %ds)\\n%s\", timeout, out.String()), nil  // ← always nil\n```\n\nThe function signature is `(string, error)` but the error is **always nil**, even when the command fails or times out. The caller in `agent.go` has dead code that checks `err != nil` from `tool.Execute()`. The actual error detection happens via string prefix matching instead. This is confusing and brittle — if the error format changes, error detection silently breaks.\n\n**Fix:** Return actual errors from `runShellCommand`, or change the signature to return only `string`.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Edit Tool Normalizes All Line Endings to `\\n` — `tools.go:133-135`\n\n```go\ncontent = strings.ReplaceAll(content, \"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\")\ncontent = strings.ReplaceAll(content, \"\\r\", \"\\n\")\n```\n\nThis silently converts **all** Windows CRLF line endings to Unix LF format when editing a file, even if the edit is just one line. This can cause issues with Windows tools that expect CRLF, and produces unnecessary diffs in version control.\n\n**Fix:** Preserve the original line ending style, or only normalize the line containing the match.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. `read` Tool Truncation at 50000 Bytes (Not Lines) — `tools.go:54-56`\n\n```go\nif len(result) \u003e 50000 {\n    result = result[:50000] + \"\\n... [truncated]\"\n}\n```\n\nThis truncates by **bytes**, not by lines or characters. For multi-byte UTF-8 content, this could break in the middle of a character, producing invalid UTF-8. The truncation message says nothing about what was truncated (unlike the shell truncation which reports line count).\n\n**Fix:** Truncate at a character boundary using `[]rune`, or use line-based truncation with a count like the shell tool.\n\n---\n\n### 🟢 LOW (Code Quality / Robustness)\n\n#### 8. Missing Nil Check on `t.tracker` in WriteTool and EditTool\n\nWhile `RecordFileWrite` handles nil receivers via `if t == nil { return }`, it's idiomatic Go to check for nil before method calls on optional dependencies. If someone adds a new method to `RollbackTracker` that doesn't have a nil check, these call sites would panic.\n\n**Fix:** Add explicit `if t.tracker != nil` checks in `WriteTool.Execute` and `EditTool.Execute`.\n\n---\n\n#### 9. `SystemMessage.SetContent` Uses `strings.TrimSpace` Inconsistently — `system.go:28-29`\n\n```go\nif m.renderWidth \u003e 0 {\n    m.renderCache = Render(SystemStyle, m.renderWidth-1, strings.TrimSpace(content))\n} else {\n    m.dirty = true\n}\n```\n\nBut in `Render()`:\n\n```go\nm.renderCache = Render(SystemStyle, width-1, m.content)  // ← no TrimSpace\n```\n\nThe cached version trims, but the normal render path does not. This inconsistency means content rendered via the cache path vs. the normal path may differ.\n\n---\n\n#### 10. `Palette.Render` Sliding Window Resets When Filter Changes\n\nWhen the filter changes, `p.index` is reset to 0, but the `Render` function's sliding window logic (`start = p.index - maxPaletteRows + 1`) doesn't account for the index reset. This is not a bug per se but means the window always resets to showing items starting at index 0 when the filter changes, which is correct behavior.\n\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: after `UpdateFilter`, if `p.index` is set to 0 but the matches list is new, `Render` works correctly. But if `UpdateFilter` sets `p.index` to 0 when it was previously negative (`p.index = -1` from the `len(p.matches) == 0` case), then later when matches become non-empty, `p.index` is still -1 and needs to be reset. Looking at the code:\n\n```go\nif len(p.matches) == 0 {\n    p.active = false\n    p.index = -1\n} else if p.index \u003c 0 || p.index \u003e= len(p.matches) {\n    p.index = 0\n}\n```\n\nThis is actually correct: if matches become non-empty and index is -1, it resets to 0.\n\n---\n\n#### 11. `palette.Args` Assumes Filter Starts After `/`\n\nThe `/` is stripped in `UpdateFilter`. But if `Args` is called with a command name that has different casing than what was typed, `EqualFold` handles it. However, `p.filter[:len(cmdName)]` could panic if `cmdName` is longer than `p.filter`. The short-circuit `len(p.filter) \u003e= len(cmdName)` prevents this, but the logic is fragile.\n\n---\n\n#### 12. Agent Context and TUI History Can Desynchronize\n\n`RollbackConversation` calls three independent truncation operations:\n- `m.agent.TruncateContextFromLastUser()` \n- `m.history.TruncateFromLastUser()`\n- `m.store.TruncateMessagesFromLastUser(m.sessionID)`\n\nIf one succeeds and another fails (e.g., agent context has a user message but TUI history doesn't), they become desynchronized. The function returns `true` if *any* of them removed messages, but the caller doesn't know which ones succeeded.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Description |\n|----------|-------|-------------|\n| 🔴 Critical | 2 | Debug panics in production, panic on malformed history |\n| 🟠 High | 2 | Sort stability data loss, fragile ToolCall/ToolResult storage |\n| 🟡 Medium | 3 | Shell error swallowed, line ending normalization, UTF-8 truncation |\n| 🟢 Low | 4 | Nil safety, inconsistent trim, desync risk, fragile Args parsing |\n\nThe most urgent fixes are: **remove the two debug panics in `thinking.go`** and **replace the panics in `ReconstructHistory` with proper error handling**."
